But if the child's lineage is not known, then you can give him your name if you want to. It's gonna come up here.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.000
It's coming up in verse 5.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.000
Do Not Change Your Names

00:18:54.000 --> 00:19:21.000
[33:5] You shall give your adopted children names that preserve their relationship to their genetic fathers. This is more equitable in the sight of GOD. If you do not know their fathers, then, as your brethren in religion, you shall treat them as members of your family. You do not commit a sin if you make a mistake in this respect; you are only responsible for your actual intentions. GOD is Forgiver, Most Merciful.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.000
I'm telling you, we are very guided people, Alhamdu Lillah, by God's grace.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:32.000
I mean, we are enjoying God's guidance because we have living examples that teach us.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:37.000
When I first... I was talking about this 10 years ago.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:45.000
And I think part of you remember that I theorized that, theoretically, a brother and sister can marry each other because of this change of names.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:48.000
They don't have their common last name.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:55.000
Because they were adopted, this girl went to this family, and this boy went to this family, and they have now their family's names,

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.000
their adopted names.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:03.000
Then, they grow up, they go to college, they meet each other, and they fall in love, and they marry.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.000
This is a brother and sister marrying each other because they don't have their names.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:08.000
You know something, this actually happened.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:15.000
And one of the people that came to the conference, I don't know if she wants me to

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.000
say this or not, but her sister married her brother.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:20.000
They actually got married.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.000
I mean, how rare can this be? What's the chance?

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:27.000
I was saying it as a theory, maybe one in a million.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:31.000
But turns out that it actually happened. I don't think she minds, but it's Mouvelle.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.000
Remember Mouvelle (unintelligible)?

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.000
One of her sisters married one of her brothers.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.000
And they found out, they broke up the marriage.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:44.000
Yeah. They found out, and they immediately separated, heartbroken and everything.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:54.000
But her sister met somebody in the market, I believe another sister, who knows the brother.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:58.000
She said "I haven't seen you in a couple of years, how are you doing?"

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.000
I said "oh, I got married, and this and that", and updated the information.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:06.000
And then, when she saw the husband, she said, "oh my gosh, this is our brother."

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:11.000
Because this is a sister that she knows, and "this is my brother." And it was, that was quite a shock.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.000
And it's because of the changing of names.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:22.000
Now, if they had the same names, if Rashad Khalifa meets Safiya Khalifa, we'll say "wait a minute, you may be my sister."

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:24.000
Dr. Khalifa: And we investigate.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:30.000
A man: So then, the offspring should have the father's name? Yes, it should have father's name.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes, should have the father's name, if the father is known.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.000
I think the commandment is very clear.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:45.000
And it's, it's difficult.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:52.000
You know, Chester for a long time, he's been known as Khalifa. He got used to the name. It's not easy to give up a thing like this.

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:57.000
It's been my experience. Douglas Brown, it took me three years to talk him out of his name.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:10.000
Dr. Khalifa: And I was, I'm laughing because he had a lot of pain that snapped him out of that name one time.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:16.000
A man: She was quite sure that the father that (unintelligible)

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:18.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yeah, the connection was unmistakable. A man: (unintelligible)

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:20.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes, did he tell you?

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:28.000
A man: Well, he told her in more detail. (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Right, okay.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yeah, the connection was very strong.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:36.000
For example, the day before, I told him straight forward, I said "Douglas, you have to change your name."

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.000
Of course, I force the name on them. I call you "Chester."

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:44.000
Chester: No, I'm laughing because you (unintelligible)

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:45.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.000
Chester: Because that's... Because it's not only (unintelligible) also.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:53.000
Chester: I think, it's, it is there also. Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:57.000
Chester: From the very beginning, it wasn't imposed on me. Dr. Khalifa: Right.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:01.000
Chester: Calling out my name. Because right from the very (unintelligible)

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Chester: first meeting near the New York (unintelligible) in New York Island, they found out my name. Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:12.000
Dr. Khalifa: (unintelligible) Chester: First of all, to tell them, you know, the day after they called me "Chester, Chester."

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.000
Dr. Khalifa: Wonderful, wonderful.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:17.000
Dr. Khalifa: This helps you. Chester: Brother, brother, (unintelligible) you know, I take it all in stride.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:22.000
Chester: At one time, I sort of resented it, but for the most part I took it in stride. Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:26.000
Chester: And it's becoming more now palatable.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes. Because we insist on calling you "Chester", sir.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.000
Dr. Khalifa: After a while you have no choice.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.000
Chester: Yes, yes, yes.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.000
Dr. Khalifa: You get used, you know, you get used to the name.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:41.000
Chester: (unintelligible) So, (unintelligible)

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:46.000
Dr. Khalifa: God rewards you for it, because what you're doing, you're giving up your personal desires for what God is saying.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:49.000
Dr. Khalifa: We all, we all do that. Chester: That's (unintelligible).

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:57.000
A woman: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.000
Dr. Khalifa: Right. What happened?

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.000
Dr. Khalifa: They tell you, ask you "what happened?"

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:04.000
A woman: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:13.000
Chester: What I'm looking at also is my family, my brothers and sisters, my mother, and my relatives and so forth.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:18.000
Because they're saying "well, it must be very awful because you're changing your name."

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:23.000
Chester: "First of all, to an Arabic name, you didn't wanna change back. And then, it's fine." Dr. Khalifa: Right.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:25.000
Chester: "What's the problem? What's wrong with that?" Dr. Khalifa: Right.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:28.000
Dr. Khalifa: You tell them. You tell them "I found out that God says."

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:30.000
Dr. Khalifa: Iman.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:39.000
Dr. Khalifa: That used to be her.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:44.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.000
Dr. Khalifa: Right.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:58.000
Iman: And at work, I had the same problem working, and it gave me an opportunity to engage with them.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:01.000
You know, I always, I've been told I had to change my name.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:07.000
And I did it because I was told I had to. But some of them has not necessarily.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:10.000
Iman: They were told to keep their name.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yeah.

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:18.000
Iman: But it was, it was a lot of conversation that made me realize (unintelligible).

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:22.000
Iman: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:28.000
Dr. Khalifa: The change is in your soul, and in your heart, the invisible part. It's not in the visible part.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:32.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:37.000
Dr. Khalifa: No, let's take, let's take Douglas Brown, for example.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:45.000
Dr. Khalifa: There's, there's a Douglas Brown senior who raised Douglas Brown Jr., for example.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:51.000
And it was heartbreaking for them, for Douglas Brown senior, to find that his son is now Rashid Hamid.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.000
There's no connection with him whatsoever.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:00.000
So, when Douglas changed back to his name, they were very happy, the parents were very happy, because they gained their son back.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:08.000
There's some kind of, shall I use the word, pride, in raising your children and having them named after you.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:13.000
It is not fair to the parents to strip them of that.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:19.000
My father is Abdulhalim Khalifa, and I'm Rashad Khalifa, and he's kind of proud that

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:20.000
you know, "I raised this son."

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.000
Then all of a sudden, I am Ali Ahmed.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:26.000
So,...

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:32.000
Yes, even the women, when they get married, should keep their names.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:41.000
But, here in this country, I don't know, there's some inconveniences that go along with that, the checking accounts, the,

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.000
I don't know, the civil laws.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:47.000
Feroz: People say they want a certain wife (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Yes. That's right.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:52.000
Feroz: (unintelligible) married and live on it. For additional security or something, they may be living together. Dr. Khalifa: Right.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:56.000
Dr. Khalifa: See, once they're married, I mean, this is common sense now.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:27:01.000
Because of the laws here, again, this will fall under the part of the Quran that says,

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:08.000
"if you're forced by circumstances, without being malicious or deliberate, then you are not committing a sin."

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:14.000
So, I will go along with changing the wife's name, if she so wills.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:19.000
A compromise would be to keep the name of the family, and the husband's family.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:23.000
For example, Batool's last name was what? Maiden name?

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.000
Huh?

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:27.000
Is this why you laugh?

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.000
Sajjadina? Sajjadina.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:32.000
Okay, so it would be Batool Sajjadina Karmally.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:34.000
Dr. Khalifa: That will be the compromise.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Feroz: So, it will be father's name?

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.000
Dr. Khalifa: Well, you guys in India and Pakistan don't have last names.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:45.000
You don't have last names.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.000
Dr. Khalifa: Really?

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:49.000
Feroz: (unintelligible)

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:54.000
Dr. Khalifa: I had a friend from Pakistan. His name was Salahuddin.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:58.000
I say "Salahuddin in Egypt, it's the first name, it's one name."

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.000
So, Salahuddin, I say "what's your last name?" He says "Salahuddin."

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:03.000
"So, what's your first name?" "Salahuddin."

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.000
"Wait a minute."

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:09.000
I say, I said "Salahuddin is your name, right? What's your last name?"

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.000
He said "this is it. We don't have last names."

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.000
Apparently, it's very common in Pakistan.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.000
Feroz: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: It is.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:20.000
A woman: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Yes. Yes.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:23.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:25.000
Feroz: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: No, Mohammad Sharif.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:29.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes. But then all of them, all of them are Mohammads.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:32.000
A woman: (unintelligible) Dr. Khalifa: Mohammad Rafiq. So,...

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:38.000
Dr. Khalifa: But it's really his first name. He's Pakistani, right?

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.000
See? You don't have last names.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.000
So, it all depends on the countries.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:46.000
A man: (unintelligible) region. Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:50.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes. A man: Those who come from Punjab. Dr. Khalifa: I don't know.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:54.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes. It's very possible. A man: (unintelligible)

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.000
A man: west Punjab and east Punjab

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.000
A man: (unintelligible)

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:08.000
A man: And those in east Punjab, they practice (unintelligible). The people who live in (unintelligible).

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:14.000
A man: Even though they are not Sikhs, they do not use the third name.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:25.000
A man: And I don't, or if they do, then they don't use (unintelligible)

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.000
Dr. Khalifa: So, it depends on the region, then.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:35.000
Dr. Khalifa: This is true all over the world, but when we conform to God's law, it's just (unintelligible) understanding.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:41.000
You have to remember not being malicious or deliberate. You're not being malicious or deliberate.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:45.000
These two things must be there to qualify for permission.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:30:11.000
[33:6] The prophet is closer to the believers than they are to each other, and his wives are like their mothers. Those who are related to each other shall take care of one another in accordance with GOD's scripture. Thus, the believers shall take care of their relatives who immigrate to them, provided they have taken care of their own families first. These are commandments of this scripture.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:18.000
Anytime you see the word Prophet, in Arabic Nabi (نَّبِى), it has to do with the local incidents

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:21.000
involving the prophet Muhammad when he was alive.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:27.000
But you can use, always use that as an example. This is what...They are the Quran. The Quran is for all times.

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.000
So, the prophet is closer to the believers than they are to each other,

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:38.000
because the prophet is receiving the Quran and delivering God's commandments to the people, which have to do with eternal existence.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:45.000
Our physical relatives are our relatives in this temporary life.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.000
If they become believers, they become our eternal family.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:51.000
Another factor enters the picture.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.000
I wanna go to the next verse, which is very important.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.000
It has to do with the covenant that the prophets made.

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:09.000
Because in the September issue of 88 Perspective, they published an article, somebody was saying

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:13.000
that Muhammad was the messenger of the covenant. Which is very wrong.

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:17.000
Because God said, 3:81, God said "He made a covenant with the prophets."

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.000
Since Muhammad was the last prophet, therefore he was included.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:25.000
God didn't say He made a covenant with the previous prophets or the prophets before Muhammad.

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:32.000
He said He made a covenant with the prophets that a messenger will come after them to unify their message.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.000
Therefore, Muhammad is not that messenger.

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.000
The messenger comes after Muhammad.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:46.000
But they established this lie that Muhammad is the last messenger, and they stick to it without any basis whatsoever. They have no basis.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:55.000
So, God wills to put in 33:7, Sura 33, which is the other end of the Quran practically,

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:58.000
that Muhammad was one of the prophets involved in that covenant.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.000
So, let us read number 7. It says,

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.000
[33:7] Recall that we took from the prophets their covenant,...

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:10.000
indicating it is one covenant. Because they will also try very hard to go to Hell and tell you "there were more than one covenant."

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.000
There wasn't. It was just one covenant.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:21.000
[33:7] ... including a pledge from you (O Muhammad), and from Noah, Abraham, Moses, and Jesus the son of Mary. We took from them a solemn pledge.*

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:24.000
And you notice everyone, every name mentioned here is a prophet.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:29.000
Because they were messengers who did not deliver scriptures like Saleh, Hud, Lot.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:34.000
You don't see Lot here, or Saleh, or Hud.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:37.000
So, these are prophets who brought scriptures.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.000
Yeah, he brought the Gospel.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:45.000
Yes.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:51.000
That was not an out-of-body experience.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:57.000
Dr. Khalifa: I had an out-of-body experience that I did deliberately. It was beautiful.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.000
A man: (unintelligible) electric.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:00.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes, anybody can do it.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.000
A man: Really? Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:03.000
Dr. Khalifa: It's the nature of things.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:09.000
Dr. Khalifa: That was, I was taken up to somewhere in the universe.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:13.000
Dr. Khalifa: No, just the prophets.

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:22.000
Feroz: I'm just wondering, like, in the universe, the body goes, isn't there a lot of forces that can affect the body?

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:24.000
Feroz: Wouldn't it be easier and faster for the soul to go? Dr. Khalifa: Yes.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:31.000
Dr. Khalifa: Well, do you think if God wants to talk to Feroz Karmally and wants the person Feroz, do you think He can take care of the body? Yeah.

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:37.000
Dr. Khalifa: You're right. There are forces, but at that time, remember, all of us have guards.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.000
Feroz: But wasn't your body just lying down there?

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:41.000
Dr. Khalifa: I was just lying in bed with the radio on my chest, listening to the Cairo.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:43.000
Feroz: So, your body then didn't go up, right?

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:44.000
Dr. Khalifa: Of course not. Feroz: Ok.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:45.000
Dr. Khalifa: It can't be.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:48.000
Feroz: It's just the soul went up. Dr. Khalifa: Yeah, the body cannot go up there.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:50.000
Feroz: OK, that's what I was saying. Dr. Khalifa: There's no way.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:52.000
Dr. Khalifa: First of all, the body needs oxygen.

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:54.000
Feroz: That's what I mean, Dr. Khalifa: Yeah.

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:58.000
Dr. Khalifa: And the minute you go up 1,000, 50,000 feet, there's no oxygen.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:01.000
Feroz: Isn't that really an out-of-body experience with regard to your body?

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:04.000
Dr. Khalifa: In a sense, yes. I mean, I'm amazed at the way Batool said it.

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:13.000
Dr. Khalifa: When you think about it, it really is an out-of-body experience. But it's a deliberate trance travel. A man: In other words, you didn't...

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:20.000
A man: you didn't deliberately, you didn't deliberately have the out-of-body experience. It was just that stuff.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:22.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes, I was summoned.

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:25.000
Dr. Khalifa: We use the word "summon."

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:30.000
A man: Now, she asked a question. (unintelligible)

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:32.000
Dr. Khalifa: Yes, I saw them all.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:40.000
I was told after the announcement that your experience in Mecca was, that was fulfillment of the covenant of all the prophets.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:42.000
I was told then that those are all the prophets.

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:48.000
When I came back, I told my father "guess what? I just met all the prophets."

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:52.000
And he said "how?" And I explained to him what I explained to you yesterday.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:54.000
I told him exactly what I saw.

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:58.000
A man: (unintelligible) thousands of...

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:01.000
